※※ Files, directories and the ring every request to them goes through.
※
※ Imported by `std` and handed on, so a program knows it as `std.fs`.  Nothing
※ here is at the top level but definitions: the ring is a field of `State`, the
※ one a program is started with is `init.fs`, and every device made from it --
※ a directory, a file, the three a process inherits -- carries where that ring
※ is.  So what reads and writes reaches the ring through what it was handed,
※ and no function here or in `std` names a variable at the top level.

※※ The ring, as the runtime keeps it.
※
※ The runtime's and not the language's: the queues the kernel shares, the
※ request each slot holds and what came back for it.  `state` says whether the
※ ring has been made yet, and whether it could be -- where it could not, the
※ runtime goes to the kernel directly and the same slots hold the answers.
※
※ The layout is the C one, because the C reads it.  The two sides are separate
※ and nothing makes them one thing, so the C says how big the record is, how it
※ is aligned and where each field lies and how wide, and a test compares.
@[abi]
type Ring =
    state : u64 ; fd : u64 ;
    sq_head : u64 ; sq_tail : u64 ; sq_mask : u64 ; sq_array : u64 ;
    sqes : u64 ;
    cq_head : u64 ; cq_tail : u64 ; cq_mask : u64 ; cqes : u64 ;
    held : u64⟦8⟧ ; answer : i64⟦8⟧

@[external("pl4g_io_submit"), io]
fn io_submit(r: &mut Ring, which: i32, fd: i32, at: u64, len: u64) → i64

@[external("pl4g_io_wait"), io]
fn io_wait(r: &mut Ring, slot: i64) → i64

@[external("pl4g_io_drain"), io]
fn io_drain(r: &mut Ring)

@[external("pl4g_io_ready"), io]
fn io_ready(r: &mut Ring, slot: i64) → i64

@[external("pl4g_io_step"), io]
fn io_step(r: &mut Ring)

@[external("pl4g_io_open"), io]
fn io_open(how: i32, dir: i32, at: u64, len: u64) → i64

@[external("pl4g_io_close"), io]
fn io_close(r: &mut Ring, fd: i32) → i64

※※ A request that has been started, and may not have been answered yet.
※
※ What it holds is where the runtime is keeping the request and which ring
※ that is, and nothing else: what the kernel said goes there too, so a request
※ the ring is still working on and one that was done on the spot are the same
※ handle read the same way.  A program that never flushes one loses nothing --
※ the drain before it ends reads every one that is left.
@[export, device]
type Pending = slot : i64 ; ring : u64

※※ What a task is handed back when it is resumed: what the kernel said about the
※※ request it waited for, as the kernel said it.  `std.done` reads it.
@[export]
type Answer = got : i64

※※ A task as `std.run` holds it: the walk, and the request it is waiting on --
※※ one whose slot is negative, before its first turn.
@[export]
type Task = walk : ⌖⇉Pending ⇇ Answer in ⎕heap ; waiting : Pending

※※ What a program does its I/O with: the ring, and the tasks `std.run` drives.
※※
※※ \details A device: a function handed one may read and write through it.  One of these is a field of what a program is started with,
※※ `init.fs`, and in the image: where it is does not change, so a device made
※※ from it carries that address and reaches the ring through it.  The ring's
※※ own descriptor is `ring.fd`, made by the runtime the first time a request
※※ is started.
@[export, device]
type State = ring : Ring ; tasks : [Task]

※※ A directory, to open files in.
※※
※※ \details `fd` is the directory's descriptor, or `AT_FDCWD` for the one the
※※ process is in; `ring` is where the ring is, which every file opened in it
※※ carries on.
@[export, device]
type Dir = fd : i32 ; ring : u64

※※ A device that can be read.
※
※ Moved and never copied, and closed with `Reader.close` -- by whatever variable
※ holds one when its scope ends, or by what it was moved on to.  The one a
※ process starts with is a field of `std.Init`, which nothing moves out of: it is
※ handed along by reference and never closed.
@[export, device, closed_by(close)]
type Reader = fd : i32 ; ring : u64

※※ A device that can be written.  Closed with `Writer.close`.
@[export, device, closed_by(close)]
type Writer = fd : i32 ; ring : u64

※※ A device that can be both, which is what a socket and a file opened either
※※ way are.  Closed with `ReadWriter.close`.
@[export, device, closed_by(close)]
type ReadWriter = fd : i32 ; ring : u64

※※ What the kernel said, where it refused, as it says it: a positive number,
※※ which is the number `⎕errno` would have been.
fn refusal(of: i64) → i32:
    ⎕narrow(0i64 - of, ⌜i32⌝) ?? 0i32

※※ The directory the process is in, to open files relative to.
※※
※※ \param s what the program does its I/O with: `&init.fs`
※※ \return the directory, whose descriptor is `AT_FDCWD` and which is never closed
@[export]
fn State.cwd(s: &State) → Dir:
    ※ `AT_FDCWD`, which is the same number on every one of these targets
    Dir(.fd ← 0i32 - 100i32, .ring ← ⎕address(&s⌖.ring))

※※ Wait for the kernel to answer something -- anything.
@[export]
fn State.step(s: &mut State):
    io_step(&mut s⌖.ring)

※※ Wait for everything outstanding.  The entry point does this when the startup
※※ function has returned; nothing stops a program doing it sooner.
@[export]
fn State.drain(s: &mut State):
    io_drain(&mut s⌖.ring)

※※ Open the file `name` in `d`, for what the type handed says: `⌜Reader⌝` to
※※ read it, `⌜Writer⌝` to write it, made if it is not there and emptied if it is,
※※ and `⌜ReadWriter⌝` for both, made if it is not there and left as it is.
※※
※※ \param d the directory `name` is relative to, where it is relative
※※ \param name the file
※※ \param kind which of the three to make
※※ \return the device, which the caller closes
※※ \raises the number the kernel gave
※
※ The type is the argument, rather than a mode beside the name, because what
※ comes back is a different type for each: a `Reader` cannot be written, which
※ is a thing the compiler can say only if it knows which was opened.  A fourth
※ kind is a fourth arm here and one more type a program may hand.  The numbers
※ handed to the runtime are the ways it knows: reading, writing and both.
@[export]
fn Dir.open(d: Dir, name: str, kind: ⌜T'⌝) → T' ? i32
        pre(⌜T'⌝ = ⌜Reader⌝ ∨ ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜Writer⌝ ∨ ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜ReadWriter⌝):
    let bytes: u8⟦⟧ = ⎕bytes(name)
    let at: u64 = if #bytes = 0: 0u64 else: ⎕address(&bytes⟦0⟧)
    comptime if ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜Reader⌝:
        let fd: i64 = io_open(0i32, d.fd, at, ⎕drop(#bytes))
        if fd < 0i64: ⊥ refusal(fd) else: Reader(.fd ← ⎕narrow(fd, ⌜i32⌝) ?? 0i32, .ring ← d.ring)
    comptime elif ⌜T'⌝ = ⌜Writer⌝:
        let fd: i64 = io_open(1i32, d.fd, at, ⎕drop(#bytes))
        if fd < 0i64: ⊥ refusal(fd) else: Writer(.fd ← ⎕narrow(fd, ⌜i32⌝) ?? 0i32, .ring ← d.ring)
    else:
        let fd: i64 = io_open(2i32, d.fd, at, ⎕drop(#bytes))
        if fd < 0i64: ⊥ refusal(fd) else: ReadWriter(.fd ← ⎕narrow(fd, ⌜i32⌝) ?? 0i32, .ring ← d.ring)

※※ Close a device that was read, once every request in flight is answered.
@[export]
fn Reader.close(r: Reader):
    _ ← io_close(⎕at(r.ring, ⌜&mut Ring⌝), r.fd)

※※ Close a device that was written, once every request in flight is answered:
※※ one may be a write to it, and what it answers is still read with `std.flush`.
@[export]
fn Writer.close(w: Writer):
    _ ← io_close(⎕at(w.ring, ⌜&mut Ring⌝), w.fd)

※※ Close a device that was both.
@[export]
fn ReadWriter.close(rw: ReadWriter):
    _ ← io_close(⎕at(rw.ring, ⌜&mut Ring⌝), rw.fd)

※※ Start reading into the `len` bytes at `at`, answering the request.
※※
※※ \raises `EAGAIN` where every slot holds an answer nobody has read
@[export]
fn Reader.start(r: &mut Reader, at: u64, len: u64) → Pending ? i32:
    ※ 0 is reading, as the runtime numbers the directions
    started(io_submit(⎕at(r⌖.ring, ⌜&mut Ring⌝), 0i32, r⌖.fd, at, len), r⌖.ring)

※※ Start writing the `len` bytes at `at`, answering the request.
※※
※※ \raises `EAGAIN` where every slot holds an answer nobody has read
@[export]
fn Writer.start(w: &mut Writer, at: u64, len: u64) → Pending ? i32:
    ※ 1 is writing
    started(io_submit(⎕at(w⌖.ring, ⌜&mut Ring⌝), 1i32, w⌖.fd, at, len), w⌖.ring)

※※ Start writing the `count` runs of bytes the kernel's `iovec`s at `at` say,
※※ as one request.
※※
※※ \raises `EAGAIN` where every slot holds an answer nobody has read
@[export]
fn Writer.start_several(w: &mut Writer, at: u64, count: u64) → Pending ? i32:
    ※ 2 is several runs written as one request
    started(io_submit(⎕at(w⌖.ring, ⌜&mut Ring⌝), 2i32, w⌖.fd, at, count), w⌖.ring)

※※ The request a slot holds, or `EAGAIN` where none could be had: every slot is
※※ holding an answer nobody has read, and what would make it work is reading one.
fn started(slot: i64, ring: u64) → Pending ? i32:
    if slot < 0i64: ⊥ 11i32 else: Pending(.slot ← slot, .ring ← ring)

※※ Wait for a request and answer what the kernel said: how many bytes went, or
※※ a negative number.
@[export]
fn Pending.wait(p: Pending) → i64:
    io_wait(⎕at(p.ring, ⌜&mut Ring⌝), p.slot)

※※ Whether a request has been answered, asked without waiting.
@[export]
fn Pending.ready(p: Pending) → bool:
    io_ready(⎕at(p.ring, ⌜&mut Ring⌝), p.slot) ≠ 0i64
